<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="tricin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>|FRAME: CPD-12572 Tricin| is a flavonoid typically distributed in sedges, palms, and grasses, including cereal crops such as rice (|FRAME: TAX-4530|), wheat (|FRAME: TAX-4565|), barley (|FRAME: TAX-4513|), maize (|FRAME: TAX-4577|), and sorghum (|FRAME: TAX-4558|) |CITS: [Zhou10]|. It is found mostly as &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-linked conjugates that serve as allelochemicals |CITS: [20730995]| and insect deterrents |CITS: [Ling07]|. |FRAME: CPD-12572 Tricin| is also found as a flavonoid monomer in monocot lignin, representing the first non-monolignol monomer involved in lignification |CITS: [25667313]|. Tricin is considered a major candidate to be developed as a nutraceutical because of its antioxidant |CITS: [17350657]|, anticancer |CITS: [17089164]|, anti-inflammatory |CITS: [22705359]|, and cardiovascular-protective properties |CITS: [20006366]|. |FRAME: CPD-12572 Tricin| is produced in rice from the flavanone |FRAME: NARINGENIN-CMPD|, which is converted into the flavone |FRAME: CPD-431|. Two enzymatically distinct flavone synthases occur in plants - the soluble Fe&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;/2-oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenase |FRAME: EC-1.14.20.5|, and the cytochrome P-450 |FRAME: EC-1.14.19.76|. It has been shown that only the latter contributes to tricin formation, forming |CITS: [26082402]|. |FRAME: CPD-431| is hydroxylated at the C-3&apos; position, followed by methylation of the hydroxyl group to form |FRAME: 574-TRIHYDROXY-3-METHOXYFLAVONE|. The main enzyme responsible for the hydroxylation is the cytochrmome P-450 |FRAME: G-58315|, as a mutation in this gene results in complete absence of |FRAME: CPD-12572| derivatives |CITS: [26082402]|. The methylation can be catalyzed by a number of &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferases, such as |FRAME: G-58316| |CITS: [26082402]| and |FRAME: G-58318| |CITS: [17943312]|. |FRAME: G-58315| then catalyzes an additional hydroxylation, this time at position 5&apos;, forming |FRAME: CPD-12571|, which is methylated to |FRAME: CPD-12572| |CITS: [26082402]|. Note that |FRAME: CPD-12572| can also be formed from |FRAME: CPD-12570| by the action of |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.175|, as described in |FRAME: PWY-6631|.</Description>
